insert into public.roots (slug, form, kind, meaning, origin, source_word, explanation) values
('e-ex','e- / ex-','prefix','out, from','Latin','Latin ex (out of)','The shorter e- and assimilated forms occur in inherited Latin compounds. The meaning may be figurative rather than spatial.'),
('re','re-','prefix','back, again','Latin','Latin re-','It can indicate return, repetition, or reversal. In inherited words its force may be less transparent than in modern English formations.'),
('com-con','com- / con- / col- / cor-','prefix','with, together; sometimes intensive','Latin','Latin com-, related to cum (with)','The final consonant changes through assimilation. Some compounds express completeness or intensity rather than literal togetherness.'),
('dis','dis- / dif- / di-','prefix','apart, away; reversal or negation','Latin','Latin dis-','It often marks separation or undoing. The di- form must not be confused with Greek di- meaning two.'),
('pro','pro-','prefix','forward, before, for','Latin and Greek','Latin pro; Greek pro (before)','Latin and Greek both supply forms with this spelling. The particular language and sense depend on the word.'),
('ob','ob- / op-','prefix','against, toward','Latin','Latin ob (toward, against)','Assimilation produces forms such as op- and oc-. Its force in inherited compounds is not always independently recoverable.'),
('de','de-','prefix','down, away, from; thoroughly','Latin','Latin de (from, down from)','It may express removal or intensification. Not every English word beginning de- contains this prefix.'),
('per','per- / pel-','prefix','through, thoroughly','Latin','Latin per (through)','The pel- variant reflects assimilation in certain Latin compounds. A word''s history determines whether the spatial or intensive sense applies.'),
('circum','circum-','prefix','around','Latin','Latin circum (around)','It can describe surrounding movement or indirect action. Figurative uses need not involve a literal circle.'),
('pre','pre- / prae-','prefix','before','Latin','Latin prae (before)','It commonly marks earlier time or advance action. The spelling pre- also occurs in some formations influenced by French.'),
('sub','sub- / suc- / sup-','prefix','under, from below','Latin','Latin sub (under)','Assimilation produces several consonant variants. Figurative meanings include subordination and assistance, not only physical position.'),
('in-into','in- / im-','prefix','in, into, on','Latin','Latin in (in, into)','This is distinct from Latin negative in-. Assimilated forms must be interpreted from the individual word''s history.'),
('ence-ance','-ence / -ance','suffix','state, quality, or action','Latin via French','Latin -entia and -antia','These endings often form nouns corresponding to participial adjectives. English spelling and French transmission can obscure the original Latin formation.'),
('tion','-tion / -ion','suffix','act, process, or result','Latin via French','Latin -tio, genitive -tionis','The visible t may belong to an inherited verbal stem rather than to a newly attached English suffix. Analysis should therefore distinguish historical stems from modern spelling divisions.'),
('ism','-ism','suffix','doctrine, practice, system, or condition','Greek via Latin and French','Greek -ismos','English extended this ending to beliefs, activities, and characteristic conditions. It does not always name an ideology.'),
('ist','-ist','suffix','one who practices, studies, or supports','Greek via Latin and French','Greek -istes','It commonly names a practitioner or adherent. In some borrowings the whole agent noun was imported rather than assembled in English.'),
('able-ible','-able / -ible','suffix','capable of, suitable for, worthy of','Latin via French','Latin -abilis and -ibilis','The choice of spelling is often inherited rather than freely interchangeable. Some words have lexical meanings narrower than simple possibility.'),
('ive','-ive','suffix','tending to, characterized by','Latin via French','Latin -ivus','It forms adjectives from verbal or nominal bases. A preceding t may belong to the inherited stem.'),
('ify','-ify','suffix','make, cause to become','Latin via French','Latin -ficare, from facere (make)','The English ending continues a Latin compound-forming element. Not every visible sequence ify can be separated mechanically.'),
('ulous','-ulous','suffix','inclined to; sometimes diminutive in origin','Latin','Latin -ulus and adjectival formations such as -ulus, -ulosus','English words grouped under this spelling have differing Latin histories. Credulous continues Latin credulus, so its ending is not simply a productive English suffix meaning very.'),
('acious','-acious','suffix','inclined to, given to','Latin via French','Latin -ax, stem -ac-, with English -ious','It often characterizes a persistent disposition. The English spelling combines an inherited Latin stem extension with an adjective ending.'),
('al','-al','suffix','relating to','Latin via French','Latin -alis','It forms relational adjectives. In some words it was added in English to an already borrowed base.'),
('y','-y / -ia','suffix','state, condition, or field','Greek, Latin, and French','Greek and Latin -ia, often through French -ie','This entry covers the learned nominal ending, not every English suffix spelled y. Its form and function depend on the borrowing history.'),
('er-or','-er / -or','suffix','a person or thing performing an action','Latin, French, and English','Latin -or and English agent -er','These agent endings have different histories despite similar functions. The individual word determines which ending is present.'),
('ary-ory','-ary / -ory','suffix','connected with; serving a function','Latin','Latin -arius and -orius','These related-looking endings have distinct Latin sources. They commonly form relational or purpose adjectives.'),
('ness','-ness','suffix','state or quality','Old English','Old English -nes, -ness','This native suffix makes abstract nouns from adjectives. It is distinct from borrowed Latin noun endings.'),
('ar','-ar','suffix','relating to','Latin','Latin -aris','This adjectival ending is related to Latin -alis. It should not be extracted from words whose final ar belongs to their root.'),
('ician','-ician','suffix','a specialist or practitioner','French and English','French -icien, combining -ique and -ien','English often attaches it to names of fields or skills. It can replace an adjectival ending rather than simply follow it.'),
('inter','inter-','prefix','between, among','Latin','Latin inter','This prefix marks intervention or a relation between participants. Its force is sometimes lexicalized in inherited compounds.'),
('ad','ad- / ac-','prefix','to, toward','Latin','Latin ad','Assimilation changes the final consonant before some bases. In accredit it contributes the idea of granting credit to someone.'),
('ab','ab- / a-','prefix','away, from','Latin','Latin ab','Its reduced form a- survives in inherited compounds. It is unrelated to Greek privative a-.'),
('equi','equi-','root','equal, same','Latin','Latin aequus','This combining element marks equality or sameness. Equivocal originally concerns the same voice or sound with differing senses.'),
('dia','dia-','prefix','through, apart','Greek','Greek dia','It can express passage or distinction. It is not the Latin prefix dis-.'),
('ana','ana-','prefix','back, again, up','Greek','Greek ana','Its meaning varies across Greek compounds. In anachronism it marks a displacement in time rather than a simple modern English operation.'),
('sis','-sis','suffix','action, process, state','Greek','Greek -sis','This ending forms Greek verbal nouns. The preceding consonants may belong to the verbal stem.'),
('syn','syn- / sym-','prefix','together, with','Greek','Greek syn','The form sym- reflects assimilation before certain consonants. It is distinct from Latin com- despite similar meanings.'),
('en','en- / em-','prefix','in, into','Greek','Greek en','The em- variant occurs before certain consonants. Empathy is a modern learned formation, not an ancient Greek psychological diagnosis.'),
('psych','psych- / psycho-','root','mind, soul','Greek','Greek psyche','Modern formations often refer to mental processes. The linking o is a compound vowel rather than a separate meaning-bearing affix.'),
('tele','tele-','root','far, at a distance','Greek','Greek tele','This element expresses distance. Its presence in telepathy says nothing about whether the claimed phenomenon exists.'),
('gen','gen-','root','produce, origin','Greek','Greek genos and the family of gignesthai','The family supplies elements concerning birth and production. In pathogenic, -genic means causing or producing.'),
('homeo','homeo-','root','similar','Greek','Greek homoios','This element means similar rather than identical. It must not be confused with home meaning a dwelling.'),
('meta','meta-','prefix','change; after, beyond','Greek','Greek meta','The force depends on the compound. In metamorphic words it concerns change of form.'),
('poly','poly-','root','many','Greek','Greek polys','This combining form indicates multiplicity. It does not by itself specify how the forms differ.'),
('zoo','zoo-','root','animal','Greek','Greek zoion','This learned element refers to animals. The combining vowel belongs to the compound formation.'),
('iso','iso-','root','equal, same','Greek','Greek isos','It indicates equality or correspondence. In mathematics the relevant sameness is structural rather than visual.'),
('meter','meter / metr','root','measure','Greek','Greek metron','The variant metr- occurs before certain endings. Time and body measurements specify the same basic measuring concept differently.'),
('soph','soph-','root','wisdom','Greek','Greek sophia and sophos','This element denotes wisdom or learning. It is not an independent English suffix.'),
('atel','atel-','root','exemption from payment','Greek','Greek ateleia','The Greek word combines privative a- with telos in its sense of tax or payment. Philately alludes to postage paid by the sender, not love of taxes.'),
('biblio','biblio-','root','book','Greek','Greek biblion','This combining form denotes books or written works. It supplies the object of affection in bibliophile.'),
('anglo','Anglo-','root','English, England','New Latin','New Latin Anglus','This combining form refers to England or English identity. Its scope is not automatically all of Britain.'),
('franco','Franco-','root','French, France','New Latin','New Latin Francus','This combining form refers to France or French culture. A francophile admires that culture rather than necessarily supporting French policy.'),
('centr','centr-','root','center','Greek via Latin','Greek kentron, Latin centrum','The original sense concerned a point or center. Modern compounds extend it to an organizing priority.'),
('contra','contra-','prefix','against','Latin','Latin contra','It marks opposition. Contradict concerns speaking against a claim rather than necessarily disproving it.'),
('jur','jur / juris','root','law, legal right','Latin','Latin ius, iuris','The stem juris is a Latin genitive form. It is not a separate English suffix in jurisdiction.'),
('vale','vale-','root','be well, farewell','Latin','Latin vale, imperative of valere','The Latin farewell literally wishes someone well. Valediction combines this farewell with saying.')
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

insert into public.words (slug, word, pronunciation, part_of_speech, definition, plain_english, examples, synonyms, antonyms, difficulty, gre_frequency, etymology, etymology_status, secondary_meanings)
select v.slug, v.slug, v.pr, case v.pos when 'n' then 'noun' when 'v' then 'verb' when 'a' then 'adjective' else 'adverb' end, v.def,
  coalesce(v.plain, initcap(v.slug) || ' means ' || lower(left(v.def,1)) || substr(v.def,2)),
  v.ex::text[], v.syn::text[], v.ant::text[], (v.df / 10)::smallint, (v.df % 10)::smallint, v.ety,
  case v.es when 'e' then 'established' when 'u' then 'uncertain' else 'none' end, v.sec::text[]
from (values
('benediction','ben-ih-DIK-shun','n','A blessing, especially one formally pronounced.',NULL,'{"The chaplain concluded the ceremony with a brief benediction.","Her mentor''s approval felt like a benediction after months of doubt."}','{"blessing","invocation"}','{"curse","malediction"}',22,'From Latin benedictio, from benedicere, combining bene (well) and dicere (say).','e','{}'),
('benefaction','ben-uh-FAK-shun','n','An act or gift that provides charitable help.',NULL,'{"The library owes its survival to a substantial benefaction.","Public praise should not be the sole motive for benefaction."}','{"gift","donation","kindness"}','{}',22,'From Latin benefactio, from bene (well) and facere (do), with the action-noun ending.','e','{}'),
('beneficial','ben-uh-FISH-ul','a','Producing a helpful or favorable effect.',NULL,'{"The reform proved beneficial to small businesses.","An intervention beneficial in trials may fail under ordinary conditions."}','{"helpful","advantageous"}','{"harmful","detrimental"}',22,'From Latin beneficialis, from beneficium (a benefit), ultimately bene (well) and facere (do).','e','{}'),
('benefit','BEN-uh-fit','n','An advantage or helpful result.',NULL,'{"The proposed merger offers little benefit to consumers.","She weighed the immediate benefit against the environmental cost."}','{"advantage","gain"}','{"disadvantage","harm"}',22,'Through French bienfait from Latin benefactum (a good deed), combining bene (well) and factum (deed).','e','{}'),
('benevolence','buh-NEV-uh-luns','n','A disposition to wish others well and help them.',NULL,'{"Private benevolence cannot replace a reliable public health system.","His benevolence extended even to his political opponents."}','{"kindness","charity"}','{"malevolence","malice"}',22,'From Latin benevolentia, from benevolens, combining bene (well), velle (wish), and an abstract-noun ending.','e','{}'),
('beneficence','buh-NEF-ih-suns','n','Active generosity or the practice of doing good.',NULL,'{"Medical ethics requires beneficence as well as respect for autonomy.","The institution advertised its beneficence while concealing discriminatory policies."}','{"charity","generosity"}','{"maleficence"}',22,'From Latin beneficentia, from beneficus (doing good), ultimately bene (well) and facere (do).','e','{}'),
('benignity','bih-NIG-nih-tee','n','Kindliness or harmlessness of character or effect.',NULL,'{"Her benignity put the anxious witnesses at ease.","The apparent benignity of the treatment encouraged further investigation."}','{"kindliness","gentleness"}','{"malignity"}',22,'From Latin benignitas, from benignus (kindly), traditionally connected with bene and an element concerning birth or kind.','e','{}'),
('benignant','bih-NIG-nunt','a','Kindly, gracious, or favorable in influence.',NULL,'{"The biography depicts a benignant ruler, despite considerable evidence of repression.","A benignant smile softened the examiner''s severe manner."}','{"benevolent","kindly"}','{"malignant"}',22,'From Latin benignus (kindly), remodeled with an adjectival ending associated with -ant; the ending is not an original participle.','e','{}'),
('beneficiary','ben-uh-FISH-ee-air-ee','n','A person entitled to receive an advantage, payment, or inheritance.',NULL,'{"Each beneficiary received an equal share of the estate.","The principal beneficiary of the subsidy was a foreign corporation."}','{"recipient","inheritor"}','{}',22,'From Latin beneficiarius, from beneficium (benefit), ultimately bene (well) and facere (do), with -arius.','e','{}'),
('benefice','BEN-uh-fis','n','A church office carrying income or property.',NULL,'{"The bishop awarded the vacant benefice to a trusted scholar.","Reformers condemned the sale of a benefice to an absentee cleric."}','{"living","ecclesiastical office"}','{}',31,'Through French from Latin beneficium (favor or grant), from bene (well) and facere (do).','e','{}'),
('benison','BEN-ih-zun','n','A blessing or expression of good wishes.',NULL,'{"The departing travelers sought the elder''s benison.","Rain arrived like a benison after the prolonged drought."}','{"blessing","benediction"}','{"curse"}',31,'From Old French beneison, from Latin benedictio (blessing); sound changes obscured dicere (say).','e','{}'),
('malignant','muh-LIG-nunt','a','Harmful, malevolent, or tending to invade surrounding tissue.',NULL,'{"The biopsy revealed a malignant tumor.","The campaign exploited a malignant strain of public resentment."}','{"harmful","virulent"}','{"benign"}',22,'From Latin malignant-, the participial stem of malignari (act maliciously), from malignus (ill-disposed).','e','{}'),
('malignancy','muh-LIG-nun-see','n','The condition of being cancerous or intensely harmful.',NULL,'{"Further tests established the lesion''s malignancy.","The essay exposes the malignancy beneath the official rhetoric."}','{"cancerousness","malevolence"}','{"benignity"}',22,'Formed from malignant with the abstract ending -ancy, corresponding to Latin-derived -ant adjectives.','e','{}'),
('malevolence','muh-LEV-uh-luns','n','A desire to cause suffering or harm.',NULL,'{"The letters revealed calculated malevolence rather than momentary anger.","She mistook his reserve for malevolence."}','{"malice","spite"}','{"benevolence"}',22,'From Latin malevolentia, combining male (ill), velle (wish), and an abstract-noun ending.','e','{}'),
('malice','MAL-is','n','The intention or desire to harm someone.',NULL,'{"The court required evidence of malice, not merely negligence.","He interpreted the criticism without attributing malice to its author."}','{"spite","ill will"}','{"goodwill","benevolence"}',22,'Through French from Latin malitia (badness), from malus (bad); not a compound with a separate English suffix -ice.','e','{}'),
('malicious','muh-LISH-us','a','Intended to hurt, damage, or cause trouble.',NULL,'{"A malicious rumor undermined confidence in the inquiry.","The software detects malicious attempts to alter confidential records."}','{"spiteful","malevolent"}','{"benevolent"}',22,'Through French from Latin malitiosus, from malitia (malice), ultimately malus (bad).','e','{}'),
('malefactor','MAL-uh-fak-ter','n','A person who commits wrongdoing or a crime.',NULL,'{"The statute punished the malefactor but ignored the injured party.","The detective resisted treating every eccentric as a potential malefactor."}','{"wrongdoer","criminal"}','{"benefactor"}',22,'From Latin malefactor, combining male (ill) and an agent noun from facere (do).','e','{}'),
('malfeasance','mal-FEE-zuns','n','Unlawful or wrongful conduct, especially by a public official.',NULL,'{"The audit uncovered evidence of official malfeasance.","Poor judgment alone does not necessarily constitute malfeasance."}','{"misconduct","wrongdoing"}','{"probity"}',22,'From French malfaisance, combining mal (badly) with a formation from faire (do) and an abstract ending.','e','{}'),
('malpractice','mal-PRAK-tis','n','Professional misconduct or negligent treatment causing harm.',NULL,'{"The surgeon faced a malpractice claim after the operation.","The report distinguished unavoidable complications from medical malpractice."}','{"negligence","misconduct"}','{}',22,'Formed in English from mal- (badly) and practice; the latter has Greek ancestry through Latin and French.','e','{}'),
('maladroit','mal-uh-DROYT','a','Clumsy or tactless in handling a situation.',NULL,'{"His maladroit apology intensified the diplomatic dispute.","A maladroit attempt at humor alienated the committee."}','{"awkward","inept"}','{"adroit","deft"}',22,'Borrowed from French maladroit, combining mal (badly) and adroit (skillful).','e','{}'),
('malcontent','MAL-kun-tent','n','A person persistently dissatisfied with existing conditions.',NULL,'{"The governor dismissed every critic as a malcontent.","The novel''s malcontent exposes injustices that respectable characters ignore."}','{"dissenter","grumbler"}','{"supporter"}',22,'From French malcontent, combining mal (badly) and content (satisfied), ultimately Latin contentus.','e','{}'),
('malnutrition','mal-noo-TRISH-un','n','Poor health caused by inadequate or unbalanced nourishment.',NULL,'{"Malnutrition impaired the children''s ability to concentrate.","The survey found malnutrition even in households with sufficient calories."}','{"undernourishment","undernutrition"}','{}',22,'Formed from mal- (badly) and nutrition, from Latin nutrire (nourish) through nutritio.','e','{}'),
('voluntary','VOL-un-tair-ee','a','Done by free choice rather than compulsion.',NULL,'{"Participation in the study was entirely voluntary.","The court questioned whether her resignation was genuinely voluntary."}','{"optional","willing"}','{"compulsory","involuntary"}',22,'From Latin voluntarius (willing), from voluntas (will), related to velle (wish), with -arius.','e','{}'),
('involuntary','in-VOL-un-tair-ee','a','Occurring without conscious choice or against one''s will.',NULL,'{"The noise elicited an involuntary start.","The policy produced an involuntary migration of skilled workers."}','{"automatic","unwilled"}','{"voluntary","deliberate"}',22,'From Latin involuntarius, combining negative in- and voluntarius (voluntary).','e','{}'),
('volunteer','vol-un-TEER','n','A person who freely offers service or assistance.',NULL,'{"A volunteer translated the documents for the refugees.","The committee assigned each volunteer a clearly defined responsibility."}','{"helper","unpaid worker"}','{}',22,'From French volontaire (voluntary or a volunteer), ultimately Latin voluntarius, with an English ending remodeled as -eer.','e','{}'),
('volitional','voh-LISH-un-ul','a','Relating to conscious choice or the exercise of will.',NULL,'{"The experiment distinguished volitional movement from reflexive response.","Her account emphasizes the volitional aspects of moral action."}','{"intentional","deliberate"}','{"reflexive","automatic"}',22,'Formed from volition and -al; volition derives ultimately from Latin velle (wish).','e','{}'),
('volitive','VOL-ih-tiv','a','Expressing a wish or involving the will.',NULL,'{"The grammarian classified the construction as volitive rather than declarative.","The philosopher distinguished volitive impulses from intellectual judgments."}','{"volitional","conative"}','{}',31,'From Medieval Latin volitivus, from a formation based on Latin velle (wish), with -ivus.','e','{}'),
('voluntarism','VOL-un-tuh-riz-um','n','A doctrine emphasizing will or voluntary action over other determining factors.',NULL,'{"Her account of moral agency rejects strict determinism in favor of voluntarism.","Critics argued that voluntarism could not sustain essential public services."}','{"volitionalism","volunteerism"}','{}',22,'Formed from Latin voluntas (will), through voluntar- formations, and -ism; its philosophical and civic senses differ.','e','{}'),
('voluntarist','VOL-un-tuh-rist','n','A supporter of a doctrine emphasizing will or voluntary cooperation.',NULL,'{"The voluntarist opposed compulsory membership in the association.","As a voluntarist, the philosopher gave willing priority over knowing."}','{"volitionalist","advocate of voluntary action"}','{}',21,'Formed from voluntarism with -ist replacing -ism; the underlying Latin noun is voluntas (will).','e','{}'),
('complacency','kum-PLAY-sun-see','n','Uncritical satisfaction that weakens vigilance or effort.',NULL,'{"Early success bred a dangerous complacency among the researchers.","The inspection exposed the costs of managerial complacency."}','{"smugness","self-satisfaction"}','{"vigilance","discontent"}',22,'From complacent with -cy, ultimately Latin complacere (please greatly), from com- and placere (please).','e','{}'),
('complacence','kum-PLAY-suns','n','Self-satisfaction, especially when accompanied by insufficient concern.',NULL,'{"The report challenges the complacence of prosperous institutions.","His complacence vanished when the financial figures appeared."}','{"complacency","smugness"}','{"unease","vigilance"}',22,'From French complaisance or related Latin complacentia; English complacence developed the self-satisfied sense.','e','{}'),
('placation','play-KAY-shun','n','The act of soothing anger or resentment.',NULL,'{"The concession was an attempt at placation rather than substantive reform.","Placation of the protesters required more than conciliatory language."}','{"appeasement","pacification"}','{"provocation"}',22,'From placate and -ion, ultimately Latin placare (soothe), related to placere (please).','e','{}'),
('placidity','pluh-SID-ih-tee','n','A condition of calmness or undisturbed tranquility.',NULL,'{"The lake''s placidity concealed powerful currents.","Her outward placidity gave no indication of the approaching confrontation."}','{"serenity","tranquility"}','{"agitation","turbulence"}',22,'From Latin placiditas, from placidus (calm), related to placere (please).','e','{}'),
('placable','PLAK-uh-bul','a','Capable of being soothed or reconciled.',NULL,'{"The negotiator discovered that the apparently furious minister was placable.","A placable opponent may still demand meaningful concessions."}','{"appeasable","conciliable"}','{"implacable"}',22,'From Latin placabilis, from placare (soothe) and -abilis.','e','{}'),
('placatory','PLAY-kuh-tor-ee','a','Intended to soothe anger or prevent resentment.',NULL,'{"The minister''s placatory remarks failed to satisfy the strikers.","Her placatory gesture opened a path toward negotiation."}','{"conciliatory","appeasing"}','{"provocative"}',22,'From Latin placatorius, from placare (soothe), with an adjective ending indicating function.','e','{}'),
('placability','plak-uh-BIL-ih-tee','n','Readiness to be soothed or reconciled.',NULL,'{"The mediator relied on the chairman''s reputation for placability.","Her placability should not be mistaken for indifference to injustice."}','{"appeasability","forgivingness"}','{"implacability"}',22,'Formed from placable with -ity, reflecting Latin placabilitas, from placare (soothe).','e','{}'),
('implacability','im-plak-uh-BIL-ih-tee','n','Unwillingness or inability to be appeased.',NULL,'{"The prosecutor''s implacability frustrated efforts to reach a settlement.","The memoir records the implacability of a decades-long feud."}','{"inexorability","relentlessness"}','{"placability"}',22,'From implacable and -ity, ultimately Latin implacabilis, with negative in- and placabilis (appeasable).','e','{}'),
('colloquial','kuh-LOH-kwee-ul','a','Characteristic of informal conversation rather than formal writing.',NULL,'{"The translator preserved the narrator''s colloquial voice.","A colloquial expression may sound inappropriate in a judicial opinion."}','{"conversational","informal"}','{"formal","literary"}',22,'From Latin colloquium (conversation), from colloqui, combining com- (together) and loqui (speak), with -al.','e','{}'),
('colloquy','KOL-uh-kwee','n','A conversation or formal exchange of views.',NULL,'{"The judges engaged counsel in a searching colloquy.","Their private colloquy produced an unexpected compromise."}','{"dialogue","discussion"}','{}',22,'From Latin colloquium, from colloqui (converse), combining com- (together) and loqui (speak).','e','{}'),
('colloquium','kuh-LOH-kwee-um','n','An academic meeting devoted to discussion of a topic.',NULL,'{"The department organized a colloquium on judicial independence.","At the colloquium, competing interpretations received equal attention."}','{"seminar","symposium"}','{}',22,'Borrowed from Latin colloquium (conversation), from com- (together) and loqui (speak). The Latin noun ending is retained.','e','{}'),
('interlocutor','in-ter-LOK-yuh-ter','n','A person participating in a conversation or dialogue.',NULL,'{"Her interlocutor challenged the distinction between consent and compliance.","A skilled interviewer allows the interlocutor time to reconsider."}','{"conversational partner","discussant"}','{}',22,'From Latin interlocutor, from interloqui (speak between), combining inter- and loqui, with an agent ending.','e','{}'),
('interlocution','in-ter-loh-KYOO-shun','n','A conversational exchange or an intervention in discussion.',NULL,'{"The philosopher preferred interlocution to uninterrupted exposition.","Their interlocution clarified a disagreement concealed by technical language."}','{"dialogue","conversation"}','{}',31,'From Latin interlocutio, from interloqui (speak between), combining inter- and loqui.','e','{}'),
('locution','loh-KYOO-shun','n','A particular expression or manner of speaking.',NULL,'{"The archaic locution puzzled readers unfamiliar with legal history.","Her preferred locution subtly shifted responsibility away from the committee."}','{"expression","phrasing"}','{}',22,'From Latin locutio (speech), from loqui (speak), with the action-noun ending.','e','{}'),
('elocution','el-uh-KYOO-shun','n','The skill or manner of clear, effective public speaking.',NULL,'{"Training in elocution improved his delivery but not his reasoning.","The academy prized precise elocution over theatrical gestures."}','{"delivery","oratory"}','{}',22,'From Latin elocutio (expression), from eloqui (speak out), combining ex- and loqui.','e','{}'),
('eloquence','EL-uh-kwuns','n','Fluent, forceful, and persuasive expression.',NULL,'{"The speech owed its eloquence to restraint rather than ornament.","Her eloquence could not compensate for the absence of evidence."}','{"expressiveness","articulacy"}','{}',22,'From Latin eloquentia, from eloqui (speak out), combining ex- and loqui.','e','{}'),
('loquacity','loh-KWAS-ih-tee','n','The tendency to talk excessively or at length.',NULL,'{"His loquacity exhausted the committee''s patience.","The memoir balances intellectual depth with an almost conversational loquacity."}','{"talkativeness","verbosity"}','{"reticence","taciturnity"}',22,'From Latin loquacitas, from loquax (talkative), derived from loqui (speak).','e','{}'),
('ventriloquist','ven-TRIL-uh-kwist','n','A performer who makes speech seem to come from elsewhere.',NULL,'{"The ventriloquist gave each puppet a distinctive political outlook.","The critic compared the novelist to a ventriloquist manipulating borrowed voices."}','{"voice thrower","puppet performer"}','{}',22,'From ventriloquy and -ist, ultimately Latin ventriloquus (belly-speaking), combining venter (belly) and loqui (speak).','e','{}'),
('ventriloquism','ven-TRIL-uh-kwiz-um','n','The practice of making one''s voice seem to originate elsewhere.',NULL,'{"Ventriloquism depends on suggestion as well as vocal control.","The essay condemns the narrator''s apparent ventriloquism of marginalized characters."}','{"voice throwing","vocal illusion"}','{}',22,'Formed from the ventriloqu- base and -ism, ultimately Latin venter (belly) and loqui (speak).','e','{}'),
('credible','KRED-uh-bul','a','Worthy of belief or reasonably convincing.',NULL,'{"The witness offered a credible explanation for the discrepancy.","A credible threat need not be explicit."}','{"believable","plausible"}','{"incredible","implausible"}',22,'From Latin credibilis, from credere (believe) and -ibilis.','e','{}'),
('incredible','in-KRED-uh-bul','a','Too extraordinary or implausible to be readily believed.',NULL,'{"The defense dismissed the allegation as incredible.","The archive revealed an incredible range of forgotten scientific proposals."}','{"unbelievable","astonishing"}','{"credible","plausible"}',22,'From Latin incredibilis, combining negative in- and credibilis (believable).','e','{}'),
('credibility','kred-uh-BIL-ih-tee','n','The quality of being believable or trustworthy.',NULL,'{"Contradictory testimony damaged the witness''s credibility.","Independent verification gave the controversial findings greater credibility."}','{"plausibility","trustworthiness"}','{"implausibility"}',22,'From credible and -ity, corresponding to Latin credibilitas, from credere (believe).','e','{}'),
('incredibility','in-kred-uh-BIL-ih-tee','n','The quality of being difficult or impossible to believe.',NULL,'{"The story''s apparent incredibility initially discouraged investigators.","The critic mistook narrative complexity for sheer incredibility."}','{"implausibility","unbelievability"}','{"credibility"}',22,'From incredible and -ity, corresponding to Latin incredibilitas, with negative in- and credere (believe).','e','{}'),
('credit','KRED-it','n','Recognition or acknowledgment of merit or responsibility.',NULL,'{"She gave her assistants full credit for the discovery.","The administration claimed credit for an economic recovery already underway."}','{"recognition","acknowledgment"}','{}',22,'Through French and Italian from Latin creditum (something entrusted), from credere (believe or trust).','e','{}'),
('discredit','dis-KRED-it','v','To damage belief in someone''s reliability or something''s validity.',NULL,'{"One flawed experiment does not discredit an entire theory.","The campaign sought to discredit the independent commission."}','{"undermine","disparage"}','{"validate","confirm"}',22,'From dis- (reversing or removing) and credit, ultimately Latin credere (believe).','e','{}'),
('accredit','uh-KRED-it','v','To recognize officially as qualified or meeting established standards.',NULL,'{"An independent agency will accredit the new medical school.","The board refused to accredit a program lacking supervised practice."}','{"certify","authorize"}','{}',22,'Through French accrediter from Italian accreditare, combining a- (to) and credito (credit), ultimately Latin credere.','e','{}'),
('accreditation','uh-kred-ih-TAY-shun','n','Official recognition that an institution meets specified standards.',NULL,'{"The college lost accreditation after repeated financial irregularities.","Accreditation does not guarantee that every course is equally rigorous."}','{"certification","authorization"}','{}',22,'Formed from accredit and -ation; accredit ultimately derives from Latin ad (to) and credere (trust).','e','{}'),
('credential','kruh-DEN-shul','n','Evidence of a person''s qualifications, authority, or entitlement.',NULL,'{"The fellowship became an important credential in her academic career.","A prestigious credential cannot substitute for demonstrated competence."}','{"qualification","certification"}','{}',22,'From Medieval Latin credentialis, from credentia (belief), ultimately credere (believe), with -alis.','e','{}'),
('credulity','kruh-DOO-lih-tee','n','Excessive readiness to believe without sufficient evidence.',NULL,'{"The fraudulent investment scheme exploited public credulity.","Scientific curiosity should not be confused with credulity."}','{"gullibility","trustfulness"}','{"skepticism","incredulity"}',22,'From Latin credulitas, from credulus (believing readily), from credere (believe).','e','{}'),
('incredulity','in-kruh-DOO-lih-tee','n','An unwillingness or inability to believe something.',NULL,'{"Her proposal was greeted with incredulity rather than careful analysis.","The witness stared at the forged signature in incredulity."}','{"disbelief","skepticism"}','{"credulity","belief"}',22,'From Latin incredulitas, from incredulus (unbelieving), combining negative in- and credulus.','e','{}'),
('creed','KREED','n','A system or formal statement of beliefs.',NULL,'{"The party''s economic creed left little room for compromise.","She rejected any creed that discouraged independent inquiry."}','{"doctrine","faith"}','{}',22,'From Old English creda, borrowed from Latin credo (I believe), the opening word of the Christian creed.','e','{}'),
('advocate','AD-vuh-kayt','v','To argue publicly in favor of a proposal or cause.',NULL,'{"The commission will advocate stricter oversight.","Few economists advocate abandoning all financial regulation."}','{"support","champion"}','{"oppose"}',22,'From Latin advocare (call to one''s aid), combining ad- and vocare (call).','e','{}'),
('evoke','ih-VOHK','v','To bring a feeling, memory, or image to mind.',NULL,'{"The ruins evoke the grandeur of a vanished empire.","A familiar melody can evoke unexpectedly vivid memories."}','{"elicit","recall"}','{}',22,'From Latin evocare (call out), combining ex- and vocare (call).','e','{}'),
('provoke','pruh-VOHK','v','To cause a reaction, especially anger or controversy.',NULL,'{"The findings provoke difficult questions about consent.","His calculated insults failed to provoke a response."}','{"incite","arouse"}','{"pacify"}',22,'From Latin provocare (call forth or challenge), combining pro- and vocare (call).','e','{}'),
('revoke','rih-VOHK','v','To withdraw or cancel an official permission or decision.',NULL,'{"The council voted to revoke the developer''s permit.","Only compelling evidence would justify a decision to revoke citizenship."}','{"rescind","cancel"}','{"grant","confirm"}',22,'From Latin revocare (call back), combining re- and vocare (call).','e','{}'),
('invoke','in-VOHK','v','To appeal to an authority, principle, or power for support.',NULL,'{"The government cannot invoke necessity to justify every restriction.","Critics invoke constitutional principles against the proposed ban."}','{"cite","appeal to"}','{}',22,'From Latin invocare (call upon), combining in- (on) and vocare (call).','e','{}'),
('vocation','voh-KAY-shun','n','A calling or occupation regarded as especially suitable or meaningful.',NULL,'{"She regarded teaching as a vocation rather than merely employment.","His vocation demanded patience as well as intellectual rigor."}','{"calling","profession"}','{}',22,'From Latin vocatio (a calling), from vocare (call) and the action-noun ending.','e','{}'),
('avocation','av-uh-KAY-shun','n','An activity pursued outside one''s main occupation.',NULL,'{"Botany remained her avocation throughout her legal career.","An avocation can provide relief from professional pressures."}','{"hobby","pastime"}','{}',22,'From Latin avocatio (a calling away), from avocare, combining ab- (away) and vocare (call).','e','{}'),
('irrevocable','ih-REV-uh-kuh-bul','a','Impossible to withdraw, reverse, or cancel.',NULL,'{"The treaty did not make the territorial settlement irrevocable.","She hesitated before taking an irrevocable step."}','{"irreversible","unalterable"}','{"revocable","reversible"}',22,'From Latin irrevocabilis, combining negative in-, re- (back), vocare (call), and -abilis.','e','{}'),
('vocal','VOH-kul','a','Expressing opinions openly and forcefully.',NULL,'{"A vocal minority opposed the proposed compromise.","The association became increasingly vocal about unsafe working conditions."}','{"outspoken","expressive"}','{"silent","reticent"}',22,'From Latin vocalis, from vox, vocis (voice), with -alis.','e','{}'),
('equivocation','ih-kwiv-uh-KAY-shun','n','Deliberately ambiguous language used to avoid commitment or conceal truth.',NULL,'{"The senator''s equivocation frustrated reporters seeking a direct answer.","Diplomatic equivocation postponed the dispute without resolving it."}','{"evasion","ambiguity"}','{"candor","forthrightness"}',22,'From equivocate and -ion, ultimately Late Latin aequivocus (having the same sound), from aequus and vox.','e','{}'),
('convoke','kun-VOHK','v','To call a group together for a formal meeting.',NULL,'{"The president may convoke an emergency session.","The charter authorizes the dean to convoke the faculty."}','{"convene","summon"}','{"disperse"}',22,'From Latin convocare (call together), combining com- and vocare (call).','e','{}'),
('diagnosis','dy-ug-NOH-sis','n','Identification of a condition or problem through examination of its features.',NULL,'{"A sound diagnosis requires more than a single observation.","The committee''s diagnosis of institutional failure was persuasive."}','{"identification","assessment"}','{}',22,'From Greek diagnosis (discernment), from dia- (apart, through) and gignoskein (know), with -sis.','e','{}'),
('prognosis','prog-NOH-sis','n','A forecast of the likely course or outcome of a condition.',NULL,'{"The prognosis improved after the patient responded to treatment.","Analysts offered a cautious prognosis for the struggling economy."}','{"forecast","outlook"}','{}',22,'From Greek prognosis (foreknowledge), combining pro- (before), a stem of gignoskein (know), and -sis.','e','{}'),
('diagnostic','dy-ug-NOS-tik','a','Useful in identifying a condition or distinguishing its nature.',NULL,'{"The physician ordered a diagnostic test.","No single feature is diagnostic of the manuscript''s date."}','{"indicative","identifying"}','{}',22,'From Greek diagnostikos, from diagignoskein (distinguish), combining dia- and gignoskein, with -ikos.','e','{}'),
('prognostic','prog-NOS-tik','a','Indicating a probable future course or outcome.',NULL,'{"The study identified a useful prognostic marker.","The committee distinguished diagnostic evidence from prognostic indicators."}','{"predictive","forecasting"}','{}',22,'From Greek prognostikos, from progignoskein (know beforehand), combining pro- and gignoskein, with -ikos.','e','{}'),
('prognosticate','prog-NOS-tih-kayt','v','To predict or foretell a future event.',NULL,'{"Few historians would prognosticate confidently about the regime''s survival.","The model can prognosticate trends but not individual decisions."}','{"predict","forecast"}','{}',22,'From Medieval Latin prognosticare, from Greek prognostikos (foreknowing), ultimately pro- and gignoskein (know).','e','{}'),
('agnosticism','ag-NOS-tih-siz-um','n','The view that certain ultimate claims, especially about God, are unknowable or unproven.',NULL,'{"Her agnosticism reflected intellectual caution rather than hostility to religion.","The essay defends agnosticism about questions that exceed available evidence."}','{"skepticism","suspension of judgment"}','{}',22,'Formed from agnostic and -ism; agnostic combines Greek a- (not) and gnostos (known).','e','{}'),
('gnostic','NOS-tik','a','Relating to spiritual knowledge or ancient movements emphasizing such knowledge.',NULL,'{"The manuscript contains gnostic interpretations of creation.","Scholars disputed whether the community''s beliefs were genuinely gnostic."}','{"esoteric","mystical"}','{}',22,'From Greek gnostikos (knowing), from gignoskein (know), with -ikos.','e','{}'),
('gnosis','NOH-sis','n','Esoteric spiritual knowledge understood as a means of liberation.',NULL,'{"The sect held that gnosis could liberate the soul.","The historian distinguished gnosis from ordinary doctrinal instruction."}','{"spiritual knowledge","illumination"}','{}',21,'From Greek gnosis (knowledge), from gignoskein (know), with -sis.','e','{}'),
('anagnorisis','an-ag-NOR-ih-sis','n','A dramatic recognition that reveals a character''s true identity or situation.',NULL,'{"The final scene turns on anagnorisis rather than physical conflict.","Her anagnorisis transforms the meaning of every earlier encounter."}','{"recognition","discovery"}','{}',31,'From Greek anagnorisis (recognition), from anagnorizein (recognize), with ana- and the family of gignoskein (know).','e','{}'),
('sympathy','SIM-puh-thee','n','Concern for another''s suffering or agreement with another''s feelings.',NULL,'{"Sympathy for the workers did not imply support for every demand.","The memoir invites sympathy without excusing the narrator''s conduct."}','{"compassion","understanding"}','{"indifference","antipathy"}',22,'From Greek sympatheia (fellow feeling), combining syn- (together) and pathos (feeling), with -eia.','e','{}'),
('pathetic','puh-THET-ik','a','Arousing pity, often through weakness, inadequacy, or distress.',NULL,'{"The defendant offered a pathetic attempt at self-justification.","The abandoned animals presented a pathetic sight."}','{"pitiful","pitiable"}','{"admirable"}',22,'From Greek pathetikos (capable of feeling or suffering), from pathein (suffer), related to pathos.','e','{}'),
('apathetic','ap-uh-THET-ik','a','Showing little interest, concern, or emotional response.',NULL,'{"An apathetic electorate allowed the proposal to pass unchallenged.","The administration remained apathetic despite mounting evidence of abuse."}','{"indifferent","unconcerned"}','{"engaged","enthusiastic"}',22,'Formed from apathy with -etic, modeled on pathetic; apathy comes from Greek a- and pathos.','e','{}'),
('empathetic','em-puh-THET-ik','a','Able to understand and share another person''s feelings.',NULL,'{"An empathetic interviewer can obtain more candid testimony.","Her empathetic response contrasted with the committee''s formal detachment."}','{"understanding","compassionate"}','{"unfeeling","insensitive"}',22,'Formed from empathy on the pattern of sympathetic; empathy uses Greek en- (in) and pathos (feeling).','e','{}'),
('sympathetic','sim-puh-THET-ik','a','Showing understanding, concern, or favorable agreement.',NULL,'{"The judge was sympathetic but constrained by the statute.","A sympathetic reading reveals the argument''s neglected strengths."}','{"compassionate","supportive"}','{"unsympathetic","hostile"}',22,'From sympathy and related Greek sympathetikos, ultimately syn- (together) and pathos (feeling).','e','{}'),
('pathos','PAY-thos','n','A quality that evokes pity or sorrow.',NULL,'{"The scene derives its pathos from the child''s restrained disappointment.","Excessive sentimentality can undermine genuine pathos."}','{"poignancy","pitifulness"}','{}',22,'Borrowed from Greek pathos (suffering, experience, emotion), without an added English suffix.','e','{}'),
('pathology','puh-THOL-uh-jee','n','The study of disease, or the abnormalities characteristic of a disease.',NULL,'{"Pathology established the cause of the unexplained deaths.","The essay treats corruption as an institutional pathology."}','{"disease science","morbid condition"}','{}',22,'From Greek pathos (suffering, disease) and -logia (study), through learned Latin formations.','e','{}'),
('psychopath','SY-koh-path','n','A person with pronounced callousness, manipulation, and antisocial traits.',NULL,'{"The novelist portrays a calculating psychopath without romanticizing his conduct.","The expert cautioned against labeling every violent offender a psychopath."}','{"callous manipulator","antisocial individual"}','{}',22,'A shortened formation related to psychopathic, combining Greek psyche (mind) and pathos (suffering or disorder); not a formal diagnosis everywhere.','e','{}'),
('telepathy','tuh-LEP-uh-thee','n','Supposed communication of thoughts without ordinary sensory contact.',NULL,'{"The experiment found no reliable evidence of telepathy.","The novel uses telepathy to question the boundaries of privacy."}','{"mind reading","thought transference"}','{}',22,'Coined from Greek tele (far away) and pathos (feeling), with the learned noun ending -y.','e','{}'),
('pathogenic','path-uh-JEN-ik','a','Capable of causing disease.',NULL,'{"The filter removes many pathogenic organisms.","Researchers identified the bacterium''s pathogenic mechanism."}','{"disease-causing","infective"}','{"nonpathogenic"}',22,'Formed from Greek pathos (disease) and a -genic element meaning producing, from genos (origin).','e','{}'),
('homeopathy','hoh-mee-OP-uh-thee','n','A treatment system using highly diluted substances selected by similarity of symptoms.',NULL,'{"The review found no robust evidence supporting homeopathy beyond placebo effects.","The lecture contrasted homeopathy with evidence-based pharmacology."}','{"homeopathic medicine","similitude therapy"}','{}',21,'From German Homöopathie, coined from Greek homoios (similar) and pathos (suffering); the name does not establish efficacy.','e','{}'),
('morphology','mor-FOL-uh-jee','n','The study of forms and structures, especially in organisms or language.',NULL,'{"The linguist studies the morphology of endangered languages.","Leaf morphology helped distinguish the closely related species."}','{"form study","structural analysis"}','{}',22,'From German Morphologie and learned formations using Greek morphe (form) and -logia (study).','e','{}'),
('morphological','mor-fuh-LOJ-ih-kul','a','Relating to the forms or structural organization of something.',NULL,'{"Morphological evidence supported the proposed classification.","The language exhibits considerable morphological complexity."}','{"structural","form-related"}','{}',22,'Formed from morphology with -ical, ultimately Greek morphe (form) and -logia (study).','e','{}')
) as v(slug, pr, pos, def, plain, ex, syn, ant, df, ety, es, sec)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;